# ADDENDUM 2 — Cohost, Path, Twitter Lists, Bluesky

## Cohost — design stance stated, never instrumented

**The thesis, verbatim** — `cohost.org/rc/welcome`, **Wayback capture 2024-01-01**: https://web.archive.org/web/20240101002259/https://cohost.org/rc/welcome (confidence: high)
> *"**algorithm? what algorithm?** all your follows' posts, in the order they were posted, in a timeline that goes vertically."*
> *"**metrics are ruining our lives** — modern social media is designed around a vicious feedback loop that keeps users Engaged at the expense of their mental health…"*
> *"**finding new things** — discovering new posts is tricky without algorithms, but we're committed to getting it right and trying new things until we get there. **curation should be done by people, not computers.**"*

They explicitly rejected the private-garden answer: *"on a web without functioning search engines, blogs and friends-only sites may be okay for some, but they leave people who are scraping by on public visibility and word of mouth in the lurch."*

**Staff's own admission of the cost** — `cohost.org/staff/post/1254822-introducing-numbers`, **2023-04-01** (an April Fools post; the framing sentence is real):
> *"**Many potential users bounce off of cohost due to a lack of metrics. They want a number that gets bigger the more they post good.**"*

**Reader-side primitives:** bookmarked tags shipped **2022-04-22** (`staff/post/5327`); tag synonyms **2024-08-18**; account silencing confirmed via staff thread `staff/post/3273958`, **2023-10-23**. Notably, a plain chronological **following feed shipped only 2023-10-23 — ~16 months after public launch.** *Tag-level* silencing: **NOT REACHED** (help.antisoftware.club Wayback captures replay as JS shells).

**The shutdown post** — `cohost.org/staff/post/7611443-cohost-to-shut-down`, jae, **2024-09-09 7:48 PM**, Wayback capture **2024-12-30**. HN: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=41492807 (136 pts). Confidence: high.
> *"lack of funding and burnout… **we have never been profitable**… policy changes from **Stripe** forced us to cancel [eggbux] earlier this year."*
> *"**We have all been on-call 24/7/365 since we launched two and a half years ago.**"*
> *"**MAU and MRR are down across the year.** We've managed to build a social media platform that many of our users love, but **we just don't have enough users and we don't have the resources to safely scale up.**"*

**Final published financials (Aug 2024)** — expenses **$41,605**, income **$16,307**, net **−$25,297**; **3,046** subscribers, MRR **$19,477**, churn 3.18%; **MAU 16,846** (−9.49% MoM); MAU→subscriber 18%. Staff commentary: *"Not great!"*
⚠️ **Caveat on the 18%:** that is MAU→subscriber, **not** registered→subscriber. Against a registered base in the low hundreds of thousands it is ~1%.

**Full arc** via lori, *"Cohost's Financials — A Retrospective"*, **2024-03-14**, https://d-shoot.net/cohostfinance.html (live; numbers quoted from staff posts; confidence medium-high for figures, medium for her interpretation — she is a critic). **Instrument warning: ASSC changed its MAU definition between Jul and Aug 2022** ("activated users" → "users who acted"), so the series is not continuous.

| Period | Registered | MAU | Subs | MRR |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Aug 2022 | 19,056 | 4,314 | 476 | $2,223 |
| Nov 2022 (Twitter exodus) | **111,128** | **37,000** | 1,852 | $8,648 |
| Dec 2022 | 118,000 | 21,000 | 2,027 | $9,440 |
| Jun 2023 | ~130,000 | **12,000** | ~1,900 | $9,008 |
| Nov 2023 | — | ~21,000 | 2,576 | $14,367 |
| Aug 2024 | — | 16,846 | 3,046 | $19,477 |

ASSC's own stated sustainability gap (June 2023): they *"would need five times the number of subscriptions they have to be sustainable, and more active users than they have total users now."*

**NOT REACHED:** individual monthly financial posts as primaries (CDX indexes them, availability API returns no snapshot); any post-shutdown post-mortem by jae/Colin — **the 2024-09-09 announcement appears to be the last word**; `blog.cohostcafe.com` (unresolvable).

**The negative finding:** ASSC published two years of financials and **never once reported tag-bookmark, silence, or following-feed usage. The design thesis was never instrumented.** Confidence: high.

## Path — **correction to the brief: the cap was 50, not 150**

**Dave Morin, "Introducing The Personal Network," blog.path.com, 2010-11-15** — **Wayback capture 2010-11-16**: https://web.archive.org/web/20101116122646/http://blog.path.com/ (confidence: high)
> *"Path allows you to capture your life's most personal moments and share them with **the 50 close friends and family** in your life who matter most."*
> *"**We chose 50 based on the research of Oxford Professor of Evolutionary Psychology Robin Dunbar**… Dunbar's research also shows that personal relationships tend to expand in factors of roughly 3… **50 is roughly the outer boundary of our personal networks.**"*
> *"**No following, no friending…just sharing with the people who matter most.**"*

**That last line matters for the brief: Path did not invert the follower graph — it abolished it.**

They pre-announced the escape hatch in the FAQ (**Wayback capture 2011-05-14**, https://web.archive.org/web/20110514002743/http://blog.path.com:80/faq):
> *"**Currently, we have chosen to focus on 50 but we may expand to 150 in the future.**"*

**The 50→150 raise: primary source NOT REACHED.** Wikipedia confirms the sequence (*"Later, Path raised its friend limit to 150 and then removed it entirely"*) but I could not date or source it. Checked and confirmed absent: Path 2.0 announcement (2011-11-29), Path 2.5 (2012-07-25), `/about` (2013-01-14), `/story` (2013-08-23). `path.com/questions` has **no Wayback snapshot**. The 2011-11-29 TechCrunch piece now 404s.

The only account of *why* is an **uncorroborated HN comment (2013-05-05) — confidence low, do not cite**: *"most active users had less then 20 friends… So they upped it to 150, and now they are seeing users in the 50 range they originally wanted."* Note the shape though: **the cap never bound**, and raising the ceiling raised realized behavior — an anchoring effect, not a capacity constraint.

**The product inverted completely by 2017** — `blog.path.com`, "Be the Inspiration," **2017-05-02** (Wayback 2018-09-20):
> *"**now anyone can be a source of inspiration**… **you can create public moments**… **your friends will stay just that – friends on Path, not public followers.** Also, you can easily turn on or turn off the public follower feature."*

2017-07-14: *"We've replaced the Activity tab with **Explore**… **public moments from millions of Path users around the world**."* Public posts, public followers, global discovery — the Dunbar product was gone in all but name.

**Shutdown** — "The Last Goodbye," **2018-09-17** (Wayback 2018-09-20): *"it is now inevitable to wind down the service to prioritize our work."* Service terminated **2018-10-18**. **No cause given.** Kakao acquired 2015-05-28; Path had turned down a **$100M Google offer in Feb 2011** and raised $2.5M + $8.5M + $30M + $25M — a VC-funded company carrying a growth-hostile constraint.

**Measured evidence the cap helped or hurt: NONE. NOT REACHED.** The only revealed evidence is the progression itself, and it is one-directional: **50 → 150 → removed → public followers. The cap was relaxed three times and never once tightened.**

## Twitter/X Lists — reader-authored, public-by-default, unmeasured

**Nick Kallen, "Soon to Launch: Lists," Twitter blog, 2009-09-30** — **Wayback capture 2016-02-07**: https://web.archive.org/web/20160207143108/https://blog.twitter.com/2009/soon-to-launch-lists (confidence: high)
> *"**The idea is to allow people to curate lists of Twitter accounts.**"*
> *"**Lists are public by default (but can be made private)**… **Other Twitter users can then subscribe to your lists. This means lists have the potential to be an important new discovery mechanism**"*
> *"We started working on this feature because of the frequent requests we received from people who were looking for a better way to organize information on Twitter."*

**Usage numbers: NOT REACHED — none located.** Twitter never published a Lists adoption figure I could find. Sysomos' "Inside Twitter" report series has **no Lists section**; a separate Sysomos Lists report has no Wayback snapshot (**NOT REACHED**).

**The academic literature treats Lists as an *instrument*, not a behavior to measure** — it studies what lists reveal about the *listed*, never how many people make them:
- Sharma et al., *"Inferring who-is-who in the Twitter social network,"* WOSN 2012, `10.1145/2342549.2342563`, 94 cites — *"can accurately and comprehensively infer attributes of millions of Twitter users, including a vast majority of Twitter's **influential** users."* The scoping to the influential tail implicitly concedes sparse coverage of the body. **PDF paywalled, NOT REACHED.**
- Rakesh et al., *"Personalized Recommendation of Twitter Lists,"* ICWSM 2014, `10.1609/icwsm.v8i1.14558` — **the existence of a recommender for Lists is itself evidence of an adoption problem.**

**Confidence caveat, stated plainly:** Semantic Scholar returned 429 on exactly the queries that would settle this. Confidence that no measurement exists: **medium**, not high.

## Bluesky — the most articulate statement of the thesis, and of its own trap

**Jay Graber, "Algorithmic choice," 2023-03-30**, https://bsky.social/about/blog/3-30-2023-algorithmic-choice (live; confidence high):
> *"**A feed of content from the people you follow ordered only by time is also an algorithm** (albeit a very simple one)… **The problem isn't 'algorithms' — the problem is a lack of transparency and control**"*
> *"We aim to replace the conventional **'master algorithm,' controlled by a single company, with an open and diverse 'marketplace of algorithms.'**"*

**"Algorithmic Choice with Custom Feeds," 2023-07-27** (updated 2023-08-17), https://bsky.social/about/blog/7-27-2023-custom-feeds:
> *"**You can also think of these feeds as super-powered hashtags or lists.**"* — Bluesky itself places feed generators in the Twitter Lists lineage.
> *"the vast majority of feeds on the network are built by independent third-party developers."*

**And in the same post, the sentence that eats the thesis:**
> *"**Even though users have a wide variety of custom feeds to choose from, defaults are still powerful.**"* — announcing `Discover`, its own opinionated default, two months after shipping choice.

**Ozone / stackable moderation, 2024-03-12**, https://bsky.social/about/blog/03-12-2024-stackable-moderation:
> *"**When you sign up for Bluesky, you will be subscribed to Bluesky's built-in moderation service by default.**"*

**The key number does not exist. Confidence: high.** Every candidate Bluesky post was fetched and grepped:
- 2023 Moderation Report (`01-16-2024`) — report volumes only.
- 2024 Moderation Report (`01-17-2025`) — same.
- **2025 Transparency Report (`01-29-2026`)** — the most detailed doc they've published: 6.97M reports (+54%), 15.49M labels (+200%), full label taxonomy (adult 10.79M, suggestive 3.42M, spam 761.77K), *"daily reports of anti-social behavior dropped by approximately 79%."* **Zero numbers on labeler subscription or custom-feed usage.** Every "choice" statistic measures *Bluesky's own moderation output*, not users exercising choice.
- Oct 2025 update announces a **"dislike" beta** — *"a new feedback signal to improve personalization in **Discover**."* Investment flows to improving the default, not to measuring migration off it.

**The one hard measured result — and it is negative.** Wang & Singhal, *"Whose Posts Get Ranked: Identical-Text Exposure Gaps in Bluesky Custom Feeds,"* **arXiv:2608.13879**, submitted **2026-08-14** (abstract read in full; confidence high):
> *"…repeated snapshots of the Top-50 lists that **1,366 public feeds** return… **in 33% of sets, one copy appears on the list while another does not.** … **Authors new to a feed receive less exposure for the same text**… **A new author with more followers than the competing author still loses 74% of head-to-head comparisons.** … These results are early evidence that **access to many independent feeds is not enough to give identical texts equal exposure.**"*

Companion: arXiv:2608.13874, panel of **5,000 monitored feeds**, 17.8M posts — useful as a scale marker; no user-side adoption data. Of the 40 most recent arXiv "Bluesky" papers (2025-10→2026-09), **none measures default-feed opt-out or labeler subscription.**

---

# 5. SYNTHESIS — what survived, what died, and what was actually measured

## The single dominant finding: **the question has never been measured.**

Across **all eight systems** — Google+ Circles, Diaspora aspects, Facebook friend lists, Twitter Lists, Path, Cohost, Mastodon lists, Bluesky feeds/labelers — **not one operator has published data on whether users do the curation work.**

- Google published one study whose denominator was self-selected weekly posters in an invite-only trial.
- Diaspora's telemetry is *structurally incapable* of answering (user/post/comment counts only).
- Twitter published nothing.
- Path published friendship counts, never cap utilization.
- Cohost published two years of financials, never a tag-bookmark number.
- Mastodon exposes no aggregate.
- Bluesky publishes a seven-decimal label taxonomy and **not one figure on labeler subscription.**

This is not an accident of my search budget — it is a consistent pattern, and it is the most defensible thing in this report. Confidence: **high**.

## What *is* measured, and it all measures the failure side

| Source | Date | Number |
|---|---|---|
| Google, Project Strobe | 2018-10-08 | *"90 percent of Google+ user sessions are less than five seconds"* |
| Stone Temple (516,246 profiles) | 2015-04-14 | **91.8%** of Google profiles never posted publicly |
| Stone Temple | 2015-04-14 | ≥50 posts ever: **0.3%** of profiles (6.66M of 2.2B) |
| fediverse.observer | 2026-09-04 | Diaspora: **1,675 MAU** / 129,502 registered ≈ **1.3%** |
| Cohost final financials | 2024-08-31 | **MAU 16,846**, −9.49% MoM, net −$25,297/mo |
| Wang & Singhal, arXiv:2608.13879 | 2026-08-14 | Reader-chosen feeds: **33%** exposure divergence on identical text; incumbency beats followers **74%** |

## The burden critique is real, primary-sourced, and predates Google+ by 10 months

The strongest evidence is **confessional, not statistical** — and it comes from the two people best positioned to know:

- **Zuckerberg, 2010-08-26**: *"**But guess what? Nobody wants to make lists.**"* (https://techcrunch.com/2010/08/26/facebook-friend-lists/)
- **Facebook official, 2011-09-13**: *"**you've told us how time-consuming it is to organize lists**… **Managing lists is boring. That's why smart lists do the work for you.**"*
- **Paul Adams, 2011-07-12**: *"**Google values technology, not social science.**"* — and his book *Social Circles* was suppressed by Google until after G+ launched.

⚠️ **Correction to the brief:** the widely-cited **"5% of users ever made a list" stat has no primary source I could find.** The related "99%" figure is **MG Siegler's rhetorical gloss in the 2010 TechCrunch piece, not a Facebook measurement.** Treat both as unsourced.

## Three structural laws the evidence supports

**1. Every survivor relaxed the constraint; none tightened it.**
Path 50→150→removed→public followers→Explore (2010–2017). Facebook manual lists → inferred Smart Lists → Friend List Feeds killed (2018-08-09). Google+ → shut down. Cohost shipped a chronological following feed only in year two and conceded *"discovering new posts is tricky without algorithms."* Bluesky shipped algorithmic choice in May 2023 and shipped `Discover` in the same announcement two months later, then began tuning it with dislikes in Oct 2025. Confidence: **high**.

**2. Defaults beat choice — stated by the party most committed to choice.** Bluesky: *"Even though users have a wide variety of custom feeds to choose from, **defaults are still powerful.**"* Facebook's answer was identical in 2011: **infer the groups, keep manual curation as an opt-in nobody must touch.**

**3. Sender-side audience tools collapse into reader-side filters.** Observed independently in four places:
- Kairam's top "Other" circle names: *church, music, tech, twitter, photographers, news* — **topical, not relational**; 43.1% of the top-1000 fell outside all relationship categories.
- Diaspora's FAQ makes aspects a read-filter as well as a write-audience.
- dredmorbius (the same **Edward Morbius** Stone Temple credits): *"The best use I've found for Circles is to group profiles very roughly by interest level, usually into 2–4 tiers."*
- Mastodon's highest-traffic list issue (#7692, 57 comments, **open since 2018-05-31**) asks for **hashtags in lists.**

## The sharpest single mechanism finding

**ActivityPub's addressable collections were built *specifically* to carry Diaspora's aspects — and the scaling optimization the whole network runs on is structurally incompatible with them.**

Christine Lemmer-Webber, AP co-editor, 2018-04-14: *"**That's the whole reason collections are addressable, in order to be able to handle the design of Diaspora's aspects.**"*
FEP-8fcf thread, 2020-12-19: *"**`bto` doesn't work with sharedInbox delivery.**"*
Mastodon's running code (`status_parser.rb`, `main`, 2026-09-04): reads `to` and `cc` only — **no `bto`, no `bcc`, and the `audience` property is not read at all.** `bto`/`bcc` appear **zero times** in Mastodon's entire AP spec-compliance document.

The capability existed for seven years at W3C Recommendation grade, was designed for exactly this use case, and the efficiency path everyone took made it unimplementable.

## Where the honest gaps are

**NOT REACHED, itemized:** Bradley Horowitz / Vic Gundotra statements · Edward Morbius' own 2015 G+ analysis (G+ is gone; only Stone Temple's credit survives) · Watson/Besmer/Lipford SOUPS 2012 (paywalled) · Kelley et al. INTERACT 2011 (paywalled) · Bernstein CHI 2013 (located, not read) · Bielenberg INFOCOM 2012 (paywalled) · Cohost monthly financials as primaries · **any Cohost post-shutdown post-mortem — appears not to exist** · Path's primary source for 50→150 · Twitter's own Lists numbers · Sysomos Lists report · Sharma WOSN 2012 coverage stats · **any operator figure anywhere on default-feed opt-out or curation-tool adoption.**

**Confidence asymmetry to carry forward:** "no measurement exists" is **high** confidence for Bluesky (every candidate doc read and grepped), Diaspora (telemetry schema inspected in source), and Mastodon (no such endpoint). It is **medium** for Twitter Lists — Semantic Scholar 429'd on precisely the queries that would settle it.

**Recommending nothing, per the brief.** One thing worth flagging for whoever writes the design doc: the recurring collapse of sender-side audience into reader-side topic filter shows up in four independent datasets, and it is the only user-behavior regularity in this entire corpus that reproduces across systems.
