# ADDENDUM — sections 2 (Diaspora), 4-partial (Mastodon, ActivityPub, Nostr)

## 2. Diaspora* "aspects" — shipped 9 months before Circles, still alive, never measured

**Origin, verbatim primary sources:**
- Developer release, **2010-09-15**, `blog.joindiaspora.com` — **Wayback capture 2010-11-27**: https://web.archive.org/web/20101127021833/http://blog.joindiaspora.com/2010/09/15/developer-release.html
  > *"We live our real lives in context, speaking from whatever aspect of ourselves that those around us know. Social tools should work the same way."*
- Private alpha, **2010-11-23** — **Wayback capture 2010-11-25**: https://web.archive.org/web/20101125104227/http://blog.joindiaspora.com/2010/11/23/private-alpha-released.html
  > *"Diaspora lets you create 'aspects,' which are personal lists that let you group people according to the roles they play in your life. We think that aspects are a simple, straightforward, lightweight way to make it really clear who is receiving your posts and who you are receiving posts from."*
- Priority claim, **2011-09-08** — **Wayback capture 2011-10-02**: https://web.archive.org/web/20111002033716/http://blog.diasporafoundation.org/2011/09/08/we-are-making-a-difference.html
  > *"We're proud that Google+ imitated one of our core features, aspects, with their circles."*

Confidence: high. Aspects predate Circles by ~9 months.

**Still there.** https://diasporafoundation.org/ (fetched 2026-09-04) still leads with them. `develop` branch retains `app/models/aspect.rb`, `AspectsController`, `AspectMembership`; `user.rb` seeds four defaults (family / friends / work / acquaintances) and carries the literal `aspect_ids == "all"` path plus a `post_default_public` flag. 202 aspect-related GitHub issues 2010→2021 — maintained, not deprecated.

**Semantics that differ from Circles** (FAQ, https://wiki.diasporafoundation.org/FAQ_for_users):
> *"Do my contacts know which aspects I have put them in? — **No. They cannot see the name of the aspect under any circumstances.**"*
> *"Are aspects like 'groups'? — **No. Sharing in diaspora\* is asymmetrical.**"*
> *"Can I restrict the posts I see to just those from certain aspects? — Yes."* — so aspects are **both** sender-side audience and reader-side filter, same dual role Circles had.
> *"Once I have posted something, can I change the aspect(s) that can see it? — No."*

**Measured aspect usage: NONE EXISTS.** Diaspora's own opt-in telemetry (`node_info_presenter.rb`) exposes exactly `total`, `active_halfyear`, `active_month`, `local_posts`, `local_comments` — **no aspect field, no public/limited split, no "all aspects" counter anywhere in the statistics surface.** Their own explainer (2014-06-04, https://blog.diasporafoundation.org/2-how-many-users-are-there-in-the-diaspora-network, Wayback 2014-07-22) concedes: *"the most we can say… is: 'at least this many'."* The one known academic measurement — Bielenberg et al., *"The growth of Diaspora"*, IEEE INFOCOM Workshops 2012, DOI `10.1109/INFCOMW.2012.6193476` — is **paywalled, NOT REACHED**. Confidence: high that no measurement is public; medium that none exists.

**Closest thing to evidence is one anecdote** — and it is the same collapse Kairam's circle-name data hinted at. HN comment, `dredmorbius`, **2021-11-18**, https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=29265456:
> *"The problem with G+ Circles (or Diaspora\* Aspects, which were an earlier version of the same thing) is that **the people and profiles in your circles have no idea how you've classified them.** … It turns out that what you actually really want are *groups*, not Circles. … **The best use I've found for Circles is to group profiles very roughly by interest level, usually into 2–4 tiers**… I've used that model on G+, Diaspora\*, and Mastodon, pretty effectively."*

Sender-side curation degraded, in practice, into a reader-side volume dial. Confidence: low as population evidence, high that the quote says this. (Note: this is almost certainly the same **Edward Morbius** credited in the Stone Temple study — `dredmorbius`.)

**Survived, barely.** `the-federation.info` returned **502** (NOT REACHED). Via `api.fediverse.observer` GraphQL, queried **2026-09-04**: **58 pods, 129,502 registered users, 1,675 monthly active, 4,817 half-year active** — roughly **1.3% MAU/registered**. Largest: diaspora-fr.org (68,541 / 379 MAU). Version 0.9.1.0 shipped early 2026. Confidence: high for the figures as reported; medium for completeness (opt-in reporting).

## 4a. Mastodon lists — reader-side only, strictly subordinate to the follow graph

https://docs.joinmastodon.org/entities/List/ (updated 2025-06-30):
> *"**Represents a list of some users that the authenticated user follows.**"*

https://docs.joinmastodon.org/methods/lists/:
> *"Add accounts to the given list. **Note that the user must be following these accounts.**"* — 422 otherwise.

https://docs.joinmastodon.org/user/network/ (updated 2025-11-27):
> *"Lists are subsets of your home timeline."*

Version history: lists shipped **2.1.0** (Nov 2017); `replies_policy` added **3.3.0**; `exclusive` (remove members from Home) added **4.2.0**. Lists are private to the owner. **There is no way to post *to* a list.** Confidence: high.

**Feature pressure, all confirmed via GitHub API — the open issues are the finding:**
- **#7876** "Add accounts to lists without having them in your main timeline" — opened 2018-06-23, closed 2023-05-20 as duplicate, 30 comments.
- **#16556** exclusive lists — opened 2021-08-02; implemented by **PR #22048** (opened 2022-12-04, merged **2023-06-05**), whose own text says *"Exclusive lists are a mature (3 year old!) Hometown feature"* — upstream took three years to adopt a fork's feature.
- **#8208** "make lists public" — opened 2018-08-15, **still open** (8 years).
- **#18292** "Shared/Collaborative User Lists" — 2022-05-03, **still open**.
- **#7692** "Ability to add hashtags to lists" — 2018-05-31, **still open**, 57 comments (highest-traffic list issue). Note the direction of demand: *topics*, not people.

**Measured list usage: NONE FOUND.** No aggregate endpoint exists. arXiv's API returned empty bodies all session (**NOT REACHED**), so third-party measurement is not ruled out. Confidence: high for Mastodon publishing nothing; low-medium that none exists.

## 4b. ActivityPub / AS2 addressing — **the claim is confirmed, and the intent is documented**

**AS2 Vocabulary §5.1** (W3C REC 2017-05-23, https://www.w3.org/TR/activitystreams-vocabulary/):
> *"Conceptually, every Object has both a Primary and Secondary audience. … The `to`, `cc`, `bto` and `bcc` properties MAY be used… **This is precisely the same model used by the `to` and `cc` fields in email systems.**"*
> *"an implementation generating an object MAY use the `bto` and `bcc` properties to list entities to whom the object should be privately targeted. When an intermediary receives an object containing these properties, it MUST remove those values prior to redistributing the object."*
> *"**Audience targeting information included within an Object only describes the intent of the object creator.** … **this specification leaves it up to implementations to determine how the audience targeting information is used.**"* ← the escape hatch that made non-use conformant.

**ActivityPub §7.1** (W3C REC 2018-01-23, https://www.w3.org/TR/activitypub/):
> *"If a recipient is a Collection or OrderedCollection, then the server MUST dereference the collection (with the user's credentials) and discover inboxes for each item in the collection."*

That collection-dereference **is** the circle/aspect primitive, and the co-editor says so outright. Christine Lemmer-Webber, HN, **2018-04-14**, https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=16836027:
> *"Co-editor of ActivityPub here. … **We also built ActivityPub so that Diaspora could use it. That's the whole reason collections are addressable, in order to be able to handle the design of Diaspora's aspects.**"*

Confidence: high. The capability was designed *specifically* for aspects, and aspects never came.

**Mastodon collapses it to five levels** — https://docs.joinmastodon.org/spec/activitypub/:
> *"**public**: … `as:Public` in `to` · **unlisted**: … `as:Public` in `cc` · **private**: … follower collection in `to` or `cc` … · **limited**: … actors in `to` or `cc`, at least one of which is not `Mention`ed in `tag` · **direct**: … all of which are `Mention`ed"*

**`bto`/`bcc` appear ZERO times in that entire document** (grep count 0, 2026-09-04). Confirmed in running code — `app/lib/activitypub/parser/status_parser.rb` on `main` reads only `audience_to` and `audience_cc`; **no `audience_bto`, no `audience_bcc`, and the `audience` property is not read at all.** Confidence: high — this is the shipped source.

**Why it died, from implementers.** `nightpool` (Mastodon core), SocialHub, **2023-01-28**, https://socialhub.activitypub.rocks/t/like-activity/2925:
> *"`to`, `cc`, `bto`, and `bcc` are primarily instructions to the *delivering* server about which inboxes to send the activity to. … Third and least their hints for the receiving client… **For activities not delivered to sharedInbox where the reason for receiving them is patently obvious, it seems like nobody really felt the need to include them.**"*

And the structural cause — FEP-8fcf thread, **2020-12-19**, https://socialhub.activitypub.rocks/t/fep-8fcf-followers-collection-synchronization-across-servers/1172:
> *"Only directly delivering messages to actors' inboxes works with `bto` and `bcc` semantics currently. **`bto` doesn't work with sharedInbox delivery**"*

**The scaling optimization the whole network runs on is structurally incompatible with blind addressing.** That is the sharpest single finding in this addendum. Confidence: high for the quote, medium that it is the decisive cause.

The W3C implementation report (https://activitypub.rocks/implementation-report/, now banner-labeled *"(Of historic interest only)"*) shows `outbox:removes-bto-and-bcc`: **5 yes of 13**, most N/A, distbin explicitly **No**. Mastodon N/A — it implements no C2S at all.

**No quantitative measurement of live `to`/`cc`/`bto`/`bcc`/`audience` usage exists.** No FEP measures it. FEP-cd47 is misleadingly named (content-addressing, not audience). Adjacent open drafts: FEP-7502 (2023-12-24), FEP-db0e (2024-05-03). A SocialHub thread *"Overlapping taxonomies and the `audience` property"* (topic 4229, **2024-05-10**) shows the community still unresolved on what `audience` means, seven years after REC. Confidence: high.

## 4c. Nostr — relay lists are routing, not audience

**NIP-65** (https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/65.md), status `draft` `optional`, from PR #218 (Mike Dilger, opened 2023-02-03, merged 2023-02-07):
> *"Defines a replaceable event using `kind:10002` to advertise relays where the user generally **writes** to and relays where the user generally **reads** mentions."*
> *"Clients SHOULD guide users to keep `kind:10002` lists small (2-4 relays of each category)."*

Its author is explicit that it is **not** access control — "The Outbox Model", https://mikedilger.com/gossip-model/ (rewritten 2024-09-26):
> *"**To be clear, this model does not apply to all the 'other stuff' on nostr. It only applies to public content.**"*
> *"It is the website model. It is the RSS model."*

And his own unresolved critique, verbatim:
> *"I can direct (via my relay list) your client to come over to a relay which you have never even heard of before, which you did not configure, and which you have not vetted. **If privacy is a concern to you, then this is a privacy attack vector.** … **If someone can come up with a relay model that is fully distributed, and lets you find everything you need to find, and doesn't have this privacy concern, PLEASE SHARE. I was unable to think one up.**"*

**Where nostr does put curation: NIP-51 Lists** (https://github.com/nostr-protocol/nips/blob/master/51.md):
> *"Lists can contain references to anything, and these references can be **public** or **private**. … private items are specified in a JSON array that mimics the structure of the event `tags` array, but stringified and **encrypted using the same scheme from NIP-44**"*

Kinds include `kind:30000` **Follow sets**, `kind:30002` **Relay sets** — and the spec's own framing is sender-side audience selection *expressed as relay choice*:
> *"relay sets can be displayed in a dropdown UI to give users the option to switch to which relays they will publish an event or from which relays they will read the replies"*

**Open tensions (GitHub API, 2026-09-04):** **#1282 "Relay Type Nomenclature"** (opened 2024-06-02, **still open**, 42 comments) proposes Private Inbox / Private Storage / Community Manager relay types — i.e. relays being asked to carry audience semantics with no agreed vocabulary. **#1179 "Follow lists don't scale"** (2024-04-16, still open, 29 comments). Meanwhile actual audience privacy went a different route entirely: **NIP-17 sealed gift-wrapped DMs** (PR #686, 2023-07-28, 178 comments — the repo's highest-traffic thread) — **encryption, not addressing.**

**NIP-65 adoption measurement: NONE.** Only the author's impression, as of 2024-09-26: *"Unfortunately few clients are creating these (at the time of writing), so these cannot be relied on either."*

---

## The cross-cutting negative finding (now confirmed across six systems)

**Every system in this study ships the curation primitive, and not one publishes — nor has had published about it — any data on whether people use it.** Diaspora's telemetry is structurally incapable of answering (user/post/comment counts only). Mastodon exposes no aggregate. The W3C AP implementation report covers 13 implementations, measures conformance not field usage, and is self-labeled historic. NIP-65 adoption is anecdote from its own author. Google published one study (Kairam CHI 2012) whose denominator was self-selected weekly posters in an invite-only trial.

**The only population-scale numbers that exist anywhere in this corpus are the ones measuring the failure**: Stone Temple's 90–91.8% never-posted, and Google's own *"90 percent of Google+ user sessions are less than five seconds."*

**And the only first-party statements about the burden are confessions, not measurements:** Zuckerberg's *"Nobody wants to make lists"* (2010-08-26), Facebook's *"you've told us how time-consuming it is"* / *"Managing lists is boring"* (2011-09-13), and Paul Adams' *"Google values technology, not social science"* (2011-07-12).

**Recurring shape worth flagging:** in three independent places the sender-side audience tool was observed collapsing into a reader-side filter — Kairam's top "Other" circle names (*church, music, tech, twitter, photographers, news*), Diaspora's FAQ making aspects a read-filter too, and dredmorbius' *"group profiles very roughly by interest level, usually into 2–4 tiers."* Mastodon's highest-traffic list issue (#7692, 57 comments) asks for exactly this: **hashtags in lists.**

**Instrument notes unchanged, plus:** `the-federation.info` **502**; arXiv API returned empty bodies to curl all session; GitHub code-search API needs auth (raw file fetch works); OpenAlex budget resets midnight UTC.

**Still pending:** Cohost, Path, Twitter/X lists, Bluesky feed generators/labelers — second agent in flight.
