# Circles as inversion of control over the social graph — comparative research

## 1. Google+ Circles

### 1a. Kairam, Brzozowski, Huffaker, Chi — "Talking in Circles: Selective Sharing in Google+" (CHI 2012) — **PRIMARY, FULL TEXT OBTAINED**

- DOI: `10.1145/2207676.2208552` · ACM: https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2207676.2208552
- Open PDF (author copy, live today): **https://sanjaykairam.com/pdf/Kairam-CHI2012.pdf**
- Published 2012-05-05. 202 citations (Semantic Scholar, checked 2026-09-04). Authors: Kairam (Stanford), Brzozowski/Huffaker/Chi (Google Inc.) — i.e. Google's own researchers.
- Confidence: **high** (read the full PDF text).

**Method (this is the load-bearing caveat):** log data from the **Google+ field trial**, week of **July 20–26, 2011** — i.e. weeks after launch, invite-only, self-selected early adopters. Sample: *"User IDs were hashed and sampled randomly from the population of users who **had shared at least once** during the data collection period, providing a representative sample of 100K active users… resulting in a data set of 64,005 users who shared content during this week."*

**Table 1 (verbatim numbers), % of users who used each option at least once / % of items:**

| Sharing | % Users | % Items |
|---|---|---|
| Public | 33.9% | 33.8% |
| Selective (Circles) | **74.8%** | **67.6%** |
| Targeted (+Mention) | 10.3% | 5.8% |

Verbatim: *"about three quarters (74.8%) of users who share during the period engaged in selective sharing using Circles at least once."*

**Critical read:** the denominator is *users who posted at least once in a week during an invite-only field trial*. It is **not** a measure of whether ordinary users will curate. Stone Temple (below) later measured that ~90% of Google profile holders **never posted publicly at all**. So 74.8% is 74.8% of a sliver.

**A buried defect finding, verbatim:** *"We note that sharing a post both publicly and to Circles functioned the same as sharing the post publicly alone; this pattern in the data signaled that a small portion of users may have been mistaken about the function of Circles."* — users misunderstood their own audience selection.

**Circle-name content analysis** (snapshot 2011-09-06, top 1,000 non-empty user-created circle names, Cohen's κ = 0.91). Percentages of circles by category:

| Rank | Work | School | Strong ties | Weak ties | Other |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Top 10 | 74.1% | 13.8% | 7.2% | 0.0% | 4.9% |
| Top 100 | 47.5% | 16.0% | 9.0% | 3.5% | 24.0% |
| Top 1000 | 31.4% | 12.6% | 8.8% | 4.0% | 43.1% |
| **All circles (lower bound)** | **10.8%** | **4.3%** | **3.0%** | **1.4%** | — |

Top-1000 names covered only **34.5% of all non-empty circles** — the long tail is idiosyncratic. Top "Other" names: *church, music, google, tech, twitter, photographers, celebrities, news, relatives* — i.e. **topical interest**, not social relation. That is a meaningful signal: a large fraction of what users built circles for was **reader-side topic filtering**, not sender-side audience control.

Default circles shipped: **Family, Friends, Acquaintances, Following**.

**Negative finding:** the paper contains **no measurement of curation burden**. `grep` for burden/effort/cumbersome/tedious/overhead returns only the framing sentence about *email*: *"the creation and maintenance of persistent groups for access in these systems can often be cumbersome or difficult."* Google's own study deliberately studied *"'expert' users"* sharing comfortably. **Nobody at Google published a measurement of the population that didn't do the work.** Confidence: high.

### 1b. Watson, Besmer, Lipford — "+Your circles: sharing behavior on Google+" (SOUPS 2012)
- DOI `10.1145/2335356.2335373` · 56 citations. **Closed access — abstract elided by publisher; Unpaywall reports `is_oa: false`, no OA location.** **NOT REACHED** (no full text, no numbers extracted).

### 1c. Stone Temple Consulting — the "ghost town" hard numbers
- Eric Enge, **"Hard Numbers for Public Posting Activity on Google Plus"**, dated **4/14/2015**.
- Live URL dead; read via **Wayback capture 2015-04-19 (timestamp 20150419171939)**: https://web.archive.org/web/20150419171939/https://www.stonetemple.com/real-numbers-for-the-activity-on-google-plus/
- Method: *"a hardcore analysis of 516,246 randomly selected Google+ profiles"*. Confidence: **high** for what they say; **medium** for the 2.2B extrapolation base.

Verbatim numbers:
- *"90 percent of the people who create a Google profile have never posted publicly on Google+."*
- *"it looks like **91.8 percent** of profiles have not posted publicly on Google+."*
- Active profiles: 49,975 of 516,246 = *"9.7 percent of all tested profiles"* → extrapolated *"more than 212 million people who have had some level of activity."*
- ≥50 public posts ever: *"0.3% percent of all tested profiles… 7.67 million users"*, adjusted **6.66 million**.
- ≥50 posts AND posted in last 30 days: adjusted **3.54 million users**.
- ≥10 posts ever: adjusted **21.8 million**. ≥5: **32.9 million**. ≥1: **111.9 million**.
- Public posting in a 30-day window: *"about 23.4 million people"*, adjusted **16M**; hyperactive (50+/month) 358K → adjusted **106K**.
- Private-activity probe (credited explicitly to **Edward Morbius**): compared 42,282 publicly-active vs 42,282 publicly-silent accounts by profile view counts. *"People showing public posts on their profile averaged more than 45,000 views per profile. People showing no public posts on their profile averaged just under 2,000 views per profile. This would suggest that the incremental usage levels of people who do no public posting at all might be about **4.3 percent**."*

**Edward Morbius' own 2015 G+ analysis: NOT REACHED** (his posts lived on Google+ itself and the platform is gone; I did not locate a mirror in the time available). Stone Temple's credit to him is the confirmed trace.

### 1d. Google's shutdown statement
- **"Project Strobe"**, Ben Smith, **2018-10-08**: https://blog.google/technology/safety-security/project-strobe/
- Verbatim: *"**90 percent of Google+ user sessions are less than five seconds.**"*
- *"while our engineering teams have put a lot of effort and dedication into building Google+ over the years, it has not achieved broad consumer or developer adoption, and has seen limited user interaction with apps."*
- Consumer shutdown over 10 months, to end of August 2019. Confidence: high.
- Consumer G+ actually shut **2019-04-02** (The Verge, https://www.theverge.com/2019/4/2/18290637/google-plus-shutdown-consumer-personal-account-delete).
- **Bradley Horowitz / Vic Gundotra individual statements: NOT REACHED** (ran out of budget before hunting them).

### 1e. Paul Adams — the research behind Circles
- **"The Real Life Social Network v2"**, SlideShare, posted **2010-07-01**, 254,482 views: https://www.slideshare.net/padday/the-real-life-social-network-v2 — Adams described as *"a UX researcher at Google who leads user research for social products."* Confidence: high (fetched).
  - Verbatim slide content: *"People tend to have between 4 and 6 groups, each of which tends to have between 2 and 10 people."*
  - *"People have multiple independent groups of friends."* / *"One friends group means mixed up conversations."*
  - Of 342 studied groups, only **3%** were actually called "friends"; **85%** didn't contain the word.
- His blog (thinkoutsidein.com, since domain-squatted; read via **Wayback**). Key related posts found in CDX: `/blog/2010/03/we-dont-have-one-group-of-friends/`, `/blog/2010/07/data-behind-real-life-social-network/`, `/blog/2011/05/small-connected-groups/`.
- **"Why I left Google. What happened to my book. What I work on at Facebook."** — **2011-07-12**, read via **Wayback capture 2011-07-15 (20110715215123)**: https://web.archive.org/web/20110715215123/http://www.thinkoutsidein.com:80/blog/2011/07/why-i-left-google-what-happened-to-my-book-what-i-work-on-at-facebook/ · Confidence: high.
  - *"Ultimately I felt that although my research formed a cornerstone of the Google social strategy, and I had correctly predicted how other products in the market would play out, I wasn't being listened to when it came to executing that strategy… **Google values technology, not social science.**"*
  - His book was titled **"Social Circles"**; *"in June 2010 they officially gave me written permission to publish it. The book content, the title, and the cover all existed prior to Emerald Sea (Google+). However, after the PR frenzy around the leaking of the project in July 2010, Google verbally rescinded permission to publish."* Reissued as **Grouped**.
  - *"Some people have wondered whether my Real Life Social Network deck was leaked. It wasn't. I first presented it months before Emerald Sea had started."*
  - Notably: at Facebook he worked on **ads**, not grouping — *"I don't work on Groups or Friend Lists."*

---

## 2. Facebook friend lists — **the strongest curation-burden evidence found**

### The Zuckerberg quote (predates Google+ by 10 months)
- MG Siegler, TechCrunch, **2010-08-26, 8:45 PM PDT**: https://techcrunch.com/2010/08/26/facebook-friend-lists/ (live, fetched directly). Confidence: **high**.
- Verbatim: *"Tonight at a Facebook Developer's Garage meeting at Facebook's headquarters in Palo Alto, Zuckerberg fielded a question about the service's privacy controls. He said that the ideal solution for sharing different things with different people is to make a friend list. **"But guess what? Nobody wants to make lists,"** Zuckerberg admitted."*
- Siegler's gloss, verbatim: *"While the idea behind friend lists is great, for the average user (in other words, 99 percent of Facebook's 500 million users) it's simply not something they're going to do. Or even if they make them at first, it's not likely something they're going to keep up with. Facebook has tried to lower the barrier to entry a few times… but they are still simply too time-consuming to set up and maintain."*
- **Important correction to the brief:** the widely-repeated "99%" is **Siegler's rhetorical figure, not a measurement**. It is not a Facebook statistic.

### The "5% of users ever made a list" stat — **SOURCE NOT FOUND**
I could not locate a primary source. Checked: TechCrunch 2011-09-08 Smart Lists piece (https://techcrunch.com/2011/09/08/facebook-begins-auto-grouping-colleagues-school-mates-and-local-friends-into-smart-lists/) — no percentage. TechCrunch 2011-09-20 Smart Lists piece — no percentage (it is a Joe Green guest post). Facebook's own launch post — no percentage. **Treat the "5%" figure as unsourced until someone finds the primary.** Confidence in the negative: medium-high.

### What Facebook *did* say, officially
- Blake Ross, **"Improved Friend Lists"**, The Facebook Blog, **2011-09-13 9:59am**, read via **Wayback capture 2011-10-01 (20111001175408)**: https://web.archive.org/web/20111001175408/http://blog.facebook.com/blog.php?post=10150278932602131 · Confidence: high.
- Verbatim: *"Lists have existed for several years, but **you've told us how time-consuming it is to organize lists for different parts of your life and keep them up to date.**"*
- Verbatim: *"**Managing lists is boring. That's why smart lists do the work for you.**"*
- Verbatim: *"**Friend Lists are completely optional. If you don't like lists, you don't have to use them.**"*
- The remedy was **automatic inference** (Work / School / Family / City smart lists from profile fields), with manual curation retained only where inference can't reach: *"Unlike your smart lists… we don't create Close Friends and Acquaintances lists for you. Since these types of relationships are unique, it's important for you to decide who goes in your Close Friends, Acquaintances or Restricted lists — if you use them at all."*
- Endpoint: Facebook **shut down Friend List Feeds** on **2018-08-09** (https://techcrunch.com/2018/08/09/facebook-is-shutting-down-friend-list-feeds-today/) — **NOT FETCHED**, HN-index only, confidence medium.

### Academic
- Kelley, Brewer, Mayer, Cranor, Sadeh — **"An Investigation into Facebook Friend Grouping"**, INTERACT 2011, LNCS. DOI `10.1007/978-3-642-23765-2_15`. **Full text NOT REACHED** (paywalled; CMU CUPS copy not found in Wayback CDX).
- Bernstein et al., **"Quantifying the invisible audience in social networks"**, CHI 2013, DOI `10.1145/2470654.2470658` (2013-04-27). **Located but full text NOT REACHED.**
- Also surfaced, not pursued: **"Are Circles Communities? A Comparative Analysis of Selective Sharing in Google+"**, ICDCSW 2014, DOI `10.1109/icdcsw.2014.34`.

---

## 3–4. Diaspora aspects, Mastodon lists, ActivityPub addressing, Nostr, Cohost, Path, Twitter lists, Bluesky
**PENDING** — two subagents are still running on these. Nothing to report from my own work; I did not duplicate their scope. One trace worth keeping from the HN index: Doug Belshaw, *"Bonfire's latest trick shows Google+ circles came a decade early"* (2022-07-30), https://dougbelshaw.com/blog/2022/07/30/bonfire-granular-boundaries/ — **NOT FETCHED**.

---

## 5. What the evidence supports so far

1. **The curation-burden critique is real, is primary-sourced, and predates Google+.** Zuckerberg said it publicly in Aug 2010; Facebook restated it as official product rationale in Sept 2011 (*"time-consuming… keep them up to date"*, *"Managing lists is boring"*). Confidence: high.
2. **Facebook's answer was to remove the curation step entirely** — infer the groups from profile data, keep manual lists as an opt-in nobody must touch. The manual half was eventually retired (Friend List Feeds, 2018).
3. **Google never published a burden measurement.** Their one public study (Kairam CHI 2012) sampled *only people already posting weekly during an invite-only trial* and found 74.8% used Circles. That number gets cited as evidence Circles worked; it is evidence that self-selected active sharers used them. Confidence: high that this is the correct reading of the method section.
4. **The population-level number is Stone Temple's:** ~90–91.8% of Google profiles never posted publicly at all. The two studies are not in conflict — they have different denominators, and the gap between them *is* the finding.
5. **A quiet counter-signal in Google's own data:** 43.1% of the top-1000 circle names fell outside work/school/strong/weak ties, and the most common of those were topical (*church, music, tech, twitter, photographers, news*). Users given a sender-side audience tool substantially repurposed it as a **reader-side topic filter**. Confidence: medium-high (the paper says *"hints that Circles based on topical interest may be common as well"* — it is a hint, not a measurement).

**Instrument notes for whoever continues this:** WebSearch budget exhausted; **OpenAlex out of daily budget** (resets midnight UTC); **Semantic Scholar rate-limits hard** (429 on unkeyed bursts, ~4s spacing works); **Google Scholar blocked**; **WebFetch refuses web.archive.org** — use `curl -sL -A "Mozilla/5.0"` for Wayback. Crossref and HN Algolia were reliable throughout.
